          "passageTitle": "Command of Evidence - Support — Q12",
          "passage": "Periods of subfreezing temperatures in Alaska\nhave been growing shorter in response to climate\nwarming, potentially enabling increased carbon\ndioxide (CO 2) absorption through greater\nproductivity of creeping dogwood (Cornus\ncanadensis) plants and other vegetation, but\nalso potentially enabling increasedCO 2 output\nthrough greater heterotrophic respiration (CO 2\ngenerated by the activity of soil\nmicroorganisms). Hydrologist Yonghong Yi and\nher colleagues developed a model incorporating\nnumerous inputs—years of solar radiation and\nsnow cover data among them—to evaluate the\neffects of warming on theCO 2 balance in\nAlaska, concluding that netCO 2 is likely to\nincrease if warming hastens spring snow melt.",
