          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 4, Q19). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t04-q020",
          "skill": "Command of Evidence - Support",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Identify textual evidence that supports a claim or hypothesis",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 4 · Q20",
          "passageTitle": "Command of Evidence - Support — Q20",
          "passage": "Water flowing around an obstruction creates\nvortices (patterns of swirls) of varying size; by\ndetecting the vortices, fish can determine the\nsize and position of the obstruction. Testing by\nYuzo R. Yanagisuru, Otar Akanyeti, and James\nC. Liao using models of three head\nshapes—narrow (low ratio of width to length),\nintermediate, and wide (high ratio of width to\nlength)—showed that for large vortices, fish with\nintermediate heads would be better able than\nwide-headed fish to distinguish between vortices\nand general turbulence in the water. A second\nresearch team has therefore hypothesized that in\nlow-visibility conditions, intermediate-headed\nfish will be more likely than wide-headed fish to\ndetect obstructions that create large vortices.",
          "prompt": "Which finding, if true, would most directly\nsupport the second research team's hypothesis?",
          "options": [
            "A study using obstructions that created\nlarge vortices in low-visibility conditions\nfound that the intermediate-headed black\nsea bass (Centropristis striates) bumped\ninto the obstructions just as often as the\nwide-headed Synodontis macropunctata\ndid.",
            "A study using obstructions that created\nlarge vortices in low-visibility conditions\nfound that the wide-headed Synodontis\nmacropunctata bumped into obstructions\nmore often than the intermediate-headed\nblack sea bass (Centropristis striates) did.",
            "A study using obstructions that created\nlarge vortices in low-visibility conditions\nfound that some specimens of the\nintermediate-headed black sea bass\n(Centropristis striates) bumped into the\nobstructions more often than other\nspecimens of the same fish did.",
            "A study using obstructions that created\nlarge vortices in low-visibility conditions\nfound that the Synodontis macropunctata,\nwhich has a relatively wide head, bumped\ninto more than half of the obstructions."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 4, Q20). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        }
      ]
